      "id": "legal-vra-1965",
      "slug": "voting-rights-act-1965",
      "title": "Voting Rights Act of 1965",
      "kind": "federal-statute",
      "lawStatus": "amended",
      "jurisdictionId": "us",
      "topics": [
        "voting",
        "constitutional"
      ],
      "citation": "Pub. L. 89-110, 79 Stat. 437",
      "sourceUrl": "https://api.congress.gov/v3/law/89/pub/110",
      "officialUrl": "https://api.congress.gov/v3/law/89/pub/110",
      "archivedCaptureUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20260717000000/https://api.congress.gov/v3/law/89/pub/110",
      "retrievedAt": "2026-07-17T00:00:00.000Z",
      "licenseTag": "public-domain",
      "factId": "BB-F-000011",
      "canonicalEntityId": "ent_law_voting_rights_act_1965",
      "explainer": {
        "whatItSays": "The Voting Rights Act of 1965 (Pub. L. 89-110) outlawed literacy tests and required federal oversight of election changes in places with a history of voting discrimination.",
        "whatItMeans": [
          "Section 2 bans any voting practice that denies or limits the right to vote based on race.",
          "Section 5 (preclearance) required covered jurisdictions to get federal approval before changing election rules — until Shelby County v. Holder (2013).",
          "Section 203 requires bilingual election materials in areas with large language-minority populations."
        ],
        "whyItMatters": [
          "Literacy tests were used for decades to block Black voters who had been denied equal education.",
          "The Act is widely credited with dramatically increasing Black voter registration in the Deep South."
        ],
        "rightsToday": [
          {
            "label": "Report a voting rights violation (DOJ Civil Rights Division)",
            "agencyUrl": "https://civilrights.justice.gov/"
          },
